4,13.79,17.75,25.64,30.31,33.25,36.26,36.51,40.72,43.26,55.15,61.78,62.45,61.74,51.61,43.09,43.92,44.53,45.59,43.36,40.02,37.08,26.97,21.76,21.9,20.11,23.87,23.54,19.66,17.52,15.62,16.17,17.06,16.18,17.63,17.97,18.21,18.55,18.55,17.69,16.4,12.5,9.96,9.53,7.44,7.79,5.05,4.3,2.01,0.57,-0.58,0.48,0.92,2.27,2.02,2.18,2.4]},"text":{"hero-eyebrow":"Year-on-year inflation, September 2026","hero-figure":"5.2<small>%</small>","hero-h1":"Back up from the lowest rate since 1999.","hero-lede":"Prices rose 5.2% in the year to September 2026. In March the rate had fallen to 3.2%, the lowest since August 1999 in the series the Ghana Statistical Service publishes today, three years after it passed 54%. Since then it has turned back up, and the rise is coming from non-food prices.","hero-fresh":"Figures to January 2026 come from GSS's StatsBank database, which hasn't been updated since 5 February 2026. February to September 2026 come from the CPI annex workbooks GSS publishes with each monthly bulletin. Where the two sources overlap, they agree to within rounding.","record-p1":"Ghana has lived through two runaway price surges in this record. The first peaked at <b>63.0% in March 2001</b>, during the cedi's collapse at the turn of the century. The second peaked at <b>54.1% in December 2022</b>, after the pandemic, the fuel shock and a debt crisis. In between, annual averages mostly ran between 7% and 20%.","turn-h2":"Food prices calmed; everything else didn't","turn-p1":"Through 2025, food drove inflation down: food prices went from rising 28% a year to under 5%. In 2026 the two halves of the basket split. Food inflation stayed between 2% and 4%, and in August food prices fell 2.6% in a single month. Non-food inflation went the other way, from 3.9% in March to 6.2% in September, and pulled the headline rate back up with it.","turn-cap":"Year-on-year inflation, national, January 2025 to September 2026. Food carries 42.7% of the basket's weight and non-food 57.3%. Source: GSS CPI annex, September 2026. This chart needs GSS's annex because StatsBank's own non-food series is broken; see the notes.","basket-cap":"Ratio of the national consumer price index in September 2026 (271.5) to the January of each start year. Index base: 2021 = 100.","drivers-p1":"At the December 2022 peak, housing and utilities prices were up 82% on a year earlier, with furnishings and transport both above 71%. Three years on, transport is the only group where prices are falling, and recreation and housing are rising fastest.","regions-p1":"The national rate hides a wide spread. In December 2022, prices in Greater Accra were up 66.7% while Volta's rose 35.6%. Six months later the order had flipped: Greater Accra had the lowest rate of all sixteen regions.","regions-p2":"In September 2026, Ashanti had the highest rate in the country, 9.8%, up from 5.0% in March. Western had the lowest, −0.5%. Upper East climbed fastest over the six months, from −1.8% to 4.5%. North East went the other way, from 8.6% to 2.8%. In Western and Bono East, prices were lower than a year earlier; in Northern they were flat.","regions-cap":"Year-on-year inflation, all products. Regional series begin with the 2021 rebase, so the first year-on-year figures are for January 2022. <b>Choose a region</b> to highlight it. January 2022 to January 2026 from StatsBank; February to September 2026 from GSS's annex.","imports-p1":"Goods Ghana imports are exposed to the exchange rate. When the cedi fell in 2022, imported-goods inflation climbed to a peak of 62.4% in January 2023, 11 points above locally produced goods. As the cedi recovered, it kept falling. In March 2026 imported goods cost 0.6% less than a year earlier, the only time in this record that either group has fallen. By September 2026 imported-goods inflation was 2.4%, against 6.4% for local goods.","imports-h2":"Imported goods rose faster, and have fallen further","imports-cap":"Year-on-year inflation, national, by source of goods, January 2022 to September 2026. Imported items carry 31.5% of the basket's weight and local items 68.5%. Source: GSS CPI annex, September 2026.","assurance-p1":"Every rate on this page was checked by recomputing it from the price index and comparing the two. How finely that check can see depends on the index's size. GSS rounds the index to one decimal place, so when it stood at 4 in 1999 the rounding alone hides nearly three percentage points. At 271 today it hides under a tenth of one.","notes-source":"Every figure comes from the <a href=\"https://statsbank.statsghana.gov.gh/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ghana Statistical Service StatsBank</a>, table <span class=\"mono\">cpi.px</span>, retrieved through its public API, and from the <a href=\"https://statsghana.gov.gh/highlights/inflation-rate\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">monthly CPI annex workbooks</a> for February to September 2026. Each value is stored with the file it came from, a fingerprint of that file and the date it was fetched.","story-foot":"Data: Ghana Statistical Service · StatsBank to Jan 2026 · CPI annexes to Sep 2026"},"extras":{"peaks":[["2001-03",63.0],["2022-12",54.1]],"turn_from":"2025-01","tiles":[["GH₵73.38","What GH₵1 of shopping in January 1998 cost in September 2026"],["GH₵9.27","What GH₵1 of shopping in January 2010 cost in September 2026"],["GH₵2.87","What GH₵1 of shopping in January 2021 cost in September 2026"]],"region_cols":["2022-12","2023-06","2026-01","2026-09"]},"review":[]}